      "claim_id": "c2",
      "status": "contradicted",
      "witness": {
        "rule": "R_INVENTED",
        "operation": "SEMANTIC",
        "quote": "the strongest factor lowering risk is that no late payments were recorded in any of the six monthly statements from April to September 2005",
        "evidence_ids": ["a1", "f1", "f2", "f3", "f4", "f5", "f6", "f7"],
        "claimed_result": "The factor 'no late payments across April-September 2005' is the strongest driver",
        "alleged_conflict": "The evidence attributes risk reduction to the derived field 'pay_max (derived)' (contribution: -0.3397), not to a composite of six PAY_* fields. The claim invents a factor ('no late payments across six months') not named in the evidence; the only attributed factor is 'pay_max (derived)', which is a single derived field summarizing the six. The claim also misattributes the driver by implying the six individual PAY_* values are the source of attribution, but only f1 (pay_max) has an attribution."
      },
      "reason": "The claim invents a factor ('no late payments across April-September 2005') not present in the evidence as an attributed feature. The evidence attributes risk reduction to 'pay_max (derived)' (a single derived field), not to the combination of six individual PAY_* facts. The claim also incorrectly implies that the six facts themselves are drivers, but only f1 has an attribution; f2–f7 are observed facts with no attribution. This violates R_INVENTED and R_FACT_AS_DRIVER (since f2–f7 have no attribution but are treated as drivers)."
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c3",
      "status": "supported",
      "witness": null,
      "reason": "The claim correctly identifies the June 2005 payment (3,000 NT dollars, from f8) and August 2005 bill (28,410 NT dollars, from f9) as positive signals. The evidence shows both have negative contributions (-0.222 and -0.1695) to the log-odds margin, meaning they decrease risk, consistent with 'positive signals'. The values and directions match exactly. No rule is violated."
    }
  ],
  "omissions": [
    {
      "evidence_ids": ["a1"],
      "reason": "The strongest attribution (in absolute value) is 'pay_max (derived)' with -0.3397, but the explanation does not name this feature. Instead, it describes a composite of six facts (f2–f7) which are not attributed. This violates R_OMISSION: the strongest attribution is not conveyed."
    }
  ]
}

