      },
      "reason": "The claim violates R_INVENTED by inventing a real-world causal explanation ('no late payments were recorded') that is not a documented feature in the model. The model attributes risk reduction to the derived feature 'pay_max (derived)', not to the aggregate of six monthly statuses. The evidence does not document that code 0 means 'no delay' — only that its meaning is 'not documented by UCI'. The claim also misrepresents the feature structure by implying multiple factors are combined, whereas the attribution is to one feature (a1)."
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c3",
      "status": "supported",
      "witness": null,
      "reason": "The claim correctly identifies the June 2005 payment (3,000 NT dollars, f8) and August 2005 bill (28,410 NT dollars, f9) as factors reducing risk. The evidence confirms these are attributed with negative log-odds contributions (-0.222 and -0.1695 respectively), and the direction 'decreases_risk' matches. No rule is violated; the phrasing 'positive signals' is a plain-language restatement of negative log-odds contributions, which is acceptable."
    }
  ],
  "omissions": [
    {
      "evidence_ids": ["a1"],
      "reason": "The strongest attribution (pay_max (derived) with -0.3397 log-odds) is not named in the explanation. While c2 attempts to describe it, it does so incorrectly by inventing a non-existent feature. The actual strongest driver is omitted from accurate identification."
